#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "81531c55-fe2e-5eff-82ba-9e08abf24a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6591" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0.$ If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =  1$  is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "3363cf38-10a2-5a55-9451-ee2101b8e809" :question/math-academy-id "e-6592" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+36{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =  6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "abc842b6-8b86-585c-a425-5776909c6425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6593" :question/problem "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$  for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+36{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root.  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x},$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b16e870-3870-5385-9745-867c9d66757d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346801} {:answer/id #uuid "11041736-130a-5b4a-95b6-a09b05e12917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346802} {:answer/id #uuid "53dee32d-3e90-578b-ad3f-c2d176db14d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346803} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15cc44-545f-500f-9073-f34dec33e3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346804} {:answer/id #uuid "73bfaebb-fa38-52be-bb87-65cf32f786d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53dee32d-3e90-578b-ad3f-c2d176db14d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346803} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb03e576-60f5-578f-b423-8382ef24a475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d136bdf-fdec-5c73-91c2-2820e0f1b6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130129" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd3dde52-172c-55fb-9943-f4d22c58b5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346808} {:answer/id #uuid "700d8184-e518-5acc-99c3-0a6b3311b1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346809} {:answer/id #uuid "649f4cc8-18be-533c-a6ba-0ba7c7b9f098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346810} {:answer/id #uuid "754647ea-823c-5165-85ad-fc4b8f2cc120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346811} {:answer/id #uuid "9231044a-d149-5888-bb6c-99ef2fd2c645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-14$ or $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754647ea-823c-5165-85ad-fc4b8f2cc120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346811} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac6990d7-e55d-54d4-87a9-00c081f07ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2739fee4-4be9-5ef3-86ba-0a7f93517649" :question/math-academy-id "q-130130" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee27f467-a18b-5301-9690-e5d96c28c11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346815} {:answer/id #uuid "b4611293-48fc-59e1-ad0a-823f007eaf5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346816} {:answer/id #uuid "fb012392-df3f-548a-8ff8-98c659db6e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346817} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f6a842-0eae-5302-933e-2c9404933c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10$ or $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346818} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0a709a-1969-5ee1-8f4d-5d76578afc1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4611293-48fc-59e1-ad0a-823f007eaf5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346816} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b30e89-c4be-5e21-8828-f82a450d9bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7199a26c-4e4c-51cd-b64d-29b5b334f42e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130133" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168af8b0-7919-5fe1-8018-7b3df237454a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346822} {:answer/id #uuid "17c7d43b-553a-57e5-bf90-f0f3fd3ae32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ or $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346823} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb7a2e9-6b56-57c0-b649-01ca45ceadf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346824} {:answer/id #uuid "8004b82e-d701-5dd8-b666-a9f7959425b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346825} {:answer/id #uuid "2262f3dc-ec31-5769-bb91-3e670aafe4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8004b82e-d701-5dd8-b666-a9f7959425b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346825} :answer-field/id #uuid "547e4665-d242-56d4-8eda-5657178d56a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e981ddc5-df31-57fd-9baf-c9afb43d6d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-130135" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee283c4-a936-5efc-85fe-7a52594e2705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346829} {:answer/id #uuid "8b853528-bee7-5883-8992-7a5df2bb81a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346830} {:answer/id #uuid "22392b02-b098-5fed-a3c3-0444442e7c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346831} {:answer/id #uuid "4d62ba0a-d4a1-5ea6-8168-938a8a1bfc91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346832} {:answer/id #uuid "289462cb-cebd-57da-a34c-8ba07b3515bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d62ba0a-d4a1-5ea6-8168-938a8a1bfc91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346832} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c2f834-a080-5dc9-9471-9f2b8f452103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2b6a362-ef37-5dff-82af-5c1a25680c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130138" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3113325-a4bb-55df-9f12-c206cf6adeb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346836} {:answer/id #uuid "771b9a3e-c0d3-55c8-93af-9dfee646b9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346837} {:answer/id #uuid "a7506024-0f8c-5574-958a-dd72464ab133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346838} {:answer/id #uuid "18ed7496-7a22-5c37-9a0d-faacda64c1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346839} {:answer/id #uuid "c315cb01-572c-5f55-9622-83dcc69effd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ed7496-7a22-5c37-9a0d-faacda64c1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346839} :answer-field/id #uuid "237bc4e1-3ad6-561f-9452-1026e9bace0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0289d6c8-b90f-54ed-b3ff-13444d54c02d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130140" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =3$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1023455-0fee-5fd5-b562-b0e975cf55e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/4}+B{e}^{3x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346843} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0771a4-2780-5bbe-8797-694295711c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346844} {:answer/id #uuid "722adcb0-c222-5527-9d41-3cea690f766f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/8}+Bx{e}^{-3x/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346845} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8b9671-d15c-56ea-b17b-205134859e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/4}+Bx{e}^{-3x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346846} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4a3931-afbf-578f-92b8-a609c3880b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/2}+B{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba8b9671-d15c-56ea-b17b-205134859e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x/4}+Bx{e}^{-3x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346846} :answer-field/id #uuid "de487a69-1719-5ed0-bb3e-103d5a8dbcc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a42ef5c-3cb8-5780-86de-2488d54e1a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-130143" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{16}y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{16}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{9}{16}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{9}{16}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4})(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/4}+Bx{e}^{-3x/4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d37650-cfb4-534e-bbf6-8bb0696c965f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5x}+x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346850} {:answer/id #uuid "e72f0bc6-486c-52c5-afbc-f75c3211f833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346851} {:answer/id #uuid "29a24edf-ef90-57e0-88de-873f1117c593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346852} {:answer/id #uuid "fba6d166-1718-5785-ad10-2138739861e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{-5x}+4x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346853} {:answer/id #uuid "584c8a30-8f6b-55f5-8a65-f22fc5aff02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584c8a30-8f6b-55f5-8a65-f22fc5aff02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7237c4bc-3505-5961-a303-3a6d43d86524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2f4662c-64fd-58fb-8db5-37499c59ff06" :question/math-academy-id "q-130146" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-5$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-5x}$ and $x{e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $10{e}^{5x}+3{e}^{-5x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a36d6561-f5dc-524b-be19-95c868cefc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346857} {:answer/id #uuid "870c019d-6b13-52f0-8cd8-ccc05b8c6631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346858} {:answer/id #uuid "8f48b359-c71d-578f-8d32-95ff1693448f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346859} {:answer/id #uuid "89fb276f-32ab-5271-8aae-4e7ff2ef0dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346860} {:answer/id #uuid "79ed494b-edcd-50fa-96a4-1a633791db28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "870c019d-6b13-52f0-8cd8-ccc05b8c6631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346858} :answer-field/id #uuid "56f9e2d7-016a-5b77-aab8-c2a7d47210af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2a1553e-9f74-54ad-8a0c-dff5b3ac1feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130147" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and $x{e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5698ffd-9ef1-566a-bb05-6d7ab7bcbc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346864} {:answer/id #uuid "f10aa9c6-8e6f-505d-8208-4dc41a526738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346865} {:answer/id #uuid "21a13ea9-1a1b-5317-b98b-56e9cf73c558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346866} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdf1f26-c527-54c6-8267-aae2bd0bf94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346867} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4a396f-05c5-54f4-b8d0-23d512aa57dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4a396f-05c5-54f4-b8d0-23d512aa57dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346868} :answer-field/id #uuid "165f98eb-f0db-5de9-87d2-0079815d2dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0fdbf32-7538-564e-a800-f0db1bc2ea85" :question/math-academy-id "q-130150" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -4)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and $x{e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34c04d1-8ebe-5ae4-80f6-25acd559073e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346871} {:answer/id #uuid "562833af-2550-58e5-8c2d-7ba21303515d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346872} {:answer/id #uuid "eec1b1da-ce82-5e71-bc2d-86c26c577c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-10$ or $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346873} {:answer/id #uuid "b982a971-5018-50c9-a954-1d06d6e3dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-20$ or $-10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346874} {:answer/id #uuid "60083595-1432-5275-9e95-fa2311d092f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ or $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d34c04d1-8ebe-5ae4-80f6-25acd559073e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346871} :answer-field/id #uuid "455a3e5b-7493-52dc-91c9-5fa4d5b7115e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd71cf56-1831-56d8-b02e-5cdd9715b655" :question/math-academy-id "q-64792" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-20{y}^{′}+100y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-20\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +100 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -10)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =10$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5d6c996-6880-5155-8e02-91204a257bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{7t} - 4te^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086018} {:answer/id #uuid "dafe28d5-e4e6-516c-aee9-29227e9392db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14e^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086019} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d1d3fc-e6d3-5217-a0f6-694b077b8d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{7t} - 2te^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086020} {:answer/id #uuid "307ad8f7-c6b6-555e-bfa0-b136a5a7745c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2te^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086021} {:answer/id #uuid "65ec4f8e-b72d-55c1-b920-606a32a5ee8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{t} + te^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ec4f8e-b72d-55c1-b920-606a32a5ee8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{t} + te^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086022} :answer-field/id #uuid "592c9c68-4d8c-509f-bb6b-2f2a364a5e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41fb4ba9-2a2a-520b-a6ad-5cdc344e0ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-64798" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8117739d-fe67-5c18-8b00-605ec83cb2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{x/2} + Bxe^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086677} {:answer/id #uuid "9f74c7cd-4e00-5a82-8956-dee7dcb99875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x/2} + Bxe^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086678} {:answer/id #uuid "b461765b-ba55-5ebf-8da3-f2df24c16e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086679} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d30fc-6522-5f8f-91f6-ea02c8846549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x/2} + Bxe^{-3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086680} {:answer/id #uuid "ebff4e66-3e32-5ba0-965a-332a76cb75dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x/2} + Be^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f74c7cd-4e00-5a82-8956-dee7dcb99875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{3x/2} + Bxe^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086678} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db49140-2925-583b-b643-9a53f11c318f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8baa66ad-4610-5025-9c74-4f7852b717ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-7795" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fb427f-cb02-59d7-959c-192dffd2cd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{9x}+Bx{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346878} {:answer/id #uuid "693be024-c6cb-50ab-bcbf-ee72a9fb47ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346879} {:answer/id #uuid "72912b0f-e6b0-51b9-a94e-02bb5e5f3f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-9x}+Bx{e}^{-9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346880} {:answer/id #uuid "84813226-c692-52fb-9004-7f61a6405305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-9x}+B{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346881} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9c13e6-3c76-555c-a710-693c7ecb6cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72912b0f-e6b0-51b9-a94e-02bb5e5f3f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-9x}+Bx{e}^{-9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346880} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e06e9f5-1a07-5293-9672-1bc4f497cc29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88499170-f6cc-5419-bb2d-57cba9139c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90957" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+18\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+81y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+18\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+81{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+18\\lambda +81) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+18\\lambda +81=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +9)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-9$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-9x}+Bx{e}^{-9x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ee3dcc-5d0a-5b14-a4a3-425a209f7f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346885} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac57af8-0fb0-5237-afdb-1dfec32540e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-3x}-6x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346886} {:answer/id #uuid "424f2958-5d08-5acb-a255-f52e9ff6f52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346887} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3bd6e-474c-5017-85b0-8fb8d8acaf3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-3x}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346888} {:answer/id #uuid "1a444750-93ef-5f82-9277-398c5a55933c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe3bd6e-474c-5017-85b0-8fb8d8acaf3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-3x}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346888} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57c8455-e834-5e70-89cd-bfef3e506c00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "640f4718-23d3-561b-85ff-76f749fae74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90965" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and $x{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-3x}+3x$ is not a solution."}]}
